# UNVERIFIED — pending debrief
# `Point::x` is the field's own NAME as a `str`, and this file is the four ways
# a program reaches for it — one right and four wrong, each with its own
# message, because they are four different mistakes.
#
# A fifth, `::` on a VARIANT, lives in its own file: it is refused one phase
# earlier, and a resolve error gates the type check, so putting it here would
# leave the other four untested while the suite stayed green.
#
# **Why the form exists, measured at panel 132.** A program that hands a field
# name to something outside this language — JSON, CSV, a query — otherwise
# types that name as a string nothing checks. A hand-written marshaller of
# three record types types **18** such strings, and changing one of them from
# `"width"` to `"witdh"` leaves `heroes build` at exit 0 with no diagnostic and
# ships the wrong key. It was caught in that experiment only because the
# program happened to read its own output back; one that only writes never
# finds out.
#
# **Why `::` and not `.`.** A prototype that reused `.` was built first and was
# green at +59 code lines, and the author chose against it: `.` already
# qualifies a module (`geom.Point`) and already reads a field from a value
# (`p.x`), so a third meaning would make the line legible only to a reader who
# knows whether the base names a type or a value, which is not on that line.
# `::` means nothing else.
#
# **The fourth case is the one this file exists for.** `Room.name`, with the
# operator this language has had all along, used to be `record_name_alone`, a
# message about how records are built — which is not the mistake somebody
# reaching for a field's name has made. It now says which repair is which, and
# carries a `certain` fix, because there is exactly one.

record Room
    name: str
    width: i64

# 1. RIGHT. The name comes from the field, so it cannot be misspelled.
function key_of_width() -> str
    return Room::width

# 2. WRONG, and the one the form exists for: a field the record does not
# declare. The message lists the fields it does have, so the repair needs no
# other file.
function slipped() -> str
    return Room::witdh  #~ no_such_field

# 3. WRONG: `::` on a value. `r` holds a room, it does not name one, and the
# message says which operator reads which.
function on_a_value(r: Room) -> str
    return r::name  #~ not_a_record_name

# 4. WRONG: the right idea with the old operator, and the field really is there.
function old_operator() -> str
    return Room.name  #~ dot_on_a_record_name

# 5. WRONG, and still the message it always was, because here the author is not
# reaching for a field name at all: no field is called `area`.
function not_even_a_field() -> str
    return Room.area  #~ record_name_alone

function main()
    print(key_of_width())
    print(slipped())
    print(on_a_value(Room(name: "hall", width: 3)))
    print(old_operator())
    print(not_even_a_field())
